<h2 id="sets-section-sets-hierarchy" class="tex-section"><span data-tag="000B">3.5</span> The hierarchy of sets</h2>

<p>
We define by transfinite recursion $V_0 = \emptyset $, $V_{\alpha + 1} = P(V_\alpha )$ (power set), and for a limit ordinal $\alpha $, 
</p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  V_\alpha = \bigcup \nolimits _{\beta < \alpha } V_\beta .  \]
</div>
<p>
 Note that each $V_\alpha $ is a transitive set. 
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-lemma" id="000C">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/000C">Lemma <span data-tag="000C">3.5.1</span>.</a> Every set is an element of $V_\alpha $ for some ordinal $\alpha $. </p>
</article> 
</p>
<article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      See <span class="cite">[<span class="postnote">Lemma 6.3</span>, <a href="/bibliography/Jech">Jech</a>]</span>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>
<p>
In <span class="cite">[<span class="postnote">Chapter III</span>, <a href="/bibliography/Kunen">Kunen</a>]</span> it is explained that this lemma is equivalent to the axiom of foundation. The <em>rank</em> of a set $S$ is the least ordinal $\alpha $ such that $S \in V_{\alpha + 1}$. By a <em>partial universe</em> we shall mean a suitably large set of the form $V_\alpha $ which will be clear from the context. 
</p>
